WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.299
In April 1985, the woman who ran a pet
cemetery in south Louisiana disappeared.

00:00:06.661 --> 00:00:10.468
She was due in court within days,
in a lawsuit over her land.

00:00:11.264 --> 00:00:15.170
Less than a month later, fishermen
found her in the Mississippi River,

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.123
weighted down with steel chains.

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:21.660
The sheriff said police had a
prime suspect from the very start:

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.201
her own groundskeeper.

00:00:23.748 --> 00:00:26.255
But there was no physical evidence.

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:28.029
No arrest.

00:00:28.554 --> 00:00:30.678
And the case went cold.

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:34.748
In 2009, a man went to the police.

00:00:35.108 --> 00:00:38.289
He said the groundskeeper
had confessed to him.

00:00:39.177 --> 00:00:41.303
And he said something else.

00:00:41.795 --> 00:00:44.512
Her ghost was haunting his dreams.

00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:48.431
Three years later,
a prison inmate came forward.

00:00:48.881 --> 00:00:51.537
He said he was there when she was killed.

00:00:52.401 --> 00:00:56.328
The case rested almost
entirely on that one witness.

00:00:56.773 --> 00:01:00.503
And in 2014, the
groundskeeper pleaded guilty.

00:01:01.003 --> 00:01:02.420
Not to murder.

00:01:02.936 --> 00:01:04.169
To manslaughter.

00:01:04.691 --> 00:01:09.169
This is the real case behind
HBO's The Pet Cemetery Murders.

00:01:09.544 --> 00:01:12.256
What the record shows,
and what it doesn't.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.045
Dorothy Thompson was sixty-one.

00:01:15.356 --> 00:01:19.065
She owned the Azalea Original
Pet Cemetery, out in Toca,

00:01:19.065 --> 00:01:21.577
in rural eastern St. Bernard Parish.

00:01:21.836 --> 00:01:25.495
Her mother, Grace, had
opened it around 1950.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:30.195
And at the centre of this story is
her land: fourteen acres in Toca.

00:01:30.565 --> 00:01:35.325
Brandon Nodier worked at the cemetery,
as its groundskeeper and handyman.

00:01:35.711 --> 00:01:40.414
According to investigators, he tricked
her into signing her land over to him.

00:01:40.832 --> 00:01:44.345
Dorothy said she'd been tricked,
and she sued to get it back.

00:01:44.614 --> 00:01:47.857
The case was heading for
a courtroom in Chalmette.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:54.298
In mid-April 1985, just days before that
court date, Dorothy Thompson disappeared.

00:01:54.702 --> 00:01:58.135
She was last seen at a bank
in eastern St. Bernard.

00:01:58.426 --> 00:02:02.031
The Sheriff's Office later said
it believed she was suffocated,

00:02:02.031 --> 00:02:04.853
inside her own home,
on the thirteenth of April.

00:02:05.748 --> 00:02:09.663
On the second of May, fishermen found
her body in the Mississippi River,

00:02:09.663 --> 00:02:11.921
on the east bank of Plaquemines Parish.

00:02:12.516 --> 00:02:15.453
She had been weighted
down with steel chains.

00:02:15.941 --> 00:02:20.647
From the start, the sheriff said,
Brandon Nodier was the prime suspect.

00:02:21.055 --> 00:02:23.896
Investigators said he wanted her land.

00:02:24.206 --> 00:02:27.413
But there was no physical
evidence tying him to her death,

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.317
and not enough to arrest anyone.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:33.068
In the early years, State Police
detectives were so stumped,

00:02:33.068 --> 00:02:35.440
they brought in a psychic from Slidell.

00:02:35.894 --> 00:02:38.055
It didn't lead to an arrest.

00:02:38.545 --> 00:02:41.410
And for years, the case went cold.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:46.662
Then Hurricane Katrina hit, and the
Sheriff's Office lost its files.

00:02:47.114 --> 00:02:49.807
But one State Police file survived.

00:02:50.074 --> 00:02:52.281
It would help rebuild the case.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:57.222
In 2009, Detective John Doran, of the
St. Bernard Parish Sheriff's Office,

00:02:57.222 --> 00:02:58.624
reopened the case.

00:02:58.994 --> 00:03:01.687
Doran said a man had
come forward, and told

00:03:01.687 --> 00:03:04.215
police that Nodier had confessed to him.

00:03:05.066 --> 00:03:09.164
And then, according to Doran,
the man said something stranger.

00:03:09.658 --> 00:03:13.112
Dorothy's ghost, he said,
was haunting his dreams.

00:03:13.784 --> 00:03:16.596
It still wasn't enough to charge anyone.

00:03:17.355 --> 00:03:22.723
Then, in 2012, according to police,
a man serving time in prison got in touch.

00:03:23.558 --> 00:03:27.318
He said he'd been Nodier's
assistant groundskeeper.

00:03:27.748 --> 00:03:31.259
And he said he was there when
Dorothy Thompson was killed.

00:03:31.856 --> 00:03:33.840
Police didn't name him.

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:38.427
He was given immunity from prosecution,
in exchange for his account.

00:03:38.755 --> 00:03:42.032
So why did he come forward,
after all that time?

00:03:42.872 --> 00:03:46.532
He said Dorothy's ghost had
started haunting him, too.

00:03:47.136 --> 00:03:51.536
But The Advocate, in New Orleans,
reported a more down-to-earth reason.

00:03:51.905 --> 00:03:56.039
It said he went to the authorities
only after Nodier visited him in jail,

00:03:56.039 --> 00:03:58.027
and threatened him and his wife.

00:03:58.553 --> 00:04:01.525
Two witnesses, and two ghost stories.

00:04:01.976 --> 00:04:05.141
But only one of them said
he saw what happened.

00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:10.480
In April 2012, a St. Bernard Parish
grand jury indicted Brandon Nodier,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.401
then fifty-eight, for
second-degree murder.

00:04:13.697 --> 00:04:16.732
He turned himself in,
and was freed on bond.

00:04:17.119 --> 00:04:19.784
It had taken twenty-seven years.

00:04:20.508 --> 00:04:23.203
The case never went to a jury.

00:04:24.075 --> 00:04:28.507
In 2014, under a plea deal,
Nodier pleaded guilty.

00:04:28.992 --> 00:04:31.742
Not to murder, but to manslaughter.

00:04:32.373 --> 00:04:33.696
He was sixty.

00:04:33.964 --> 00:04:36.801
He was sentenced to ten years in prison.

00:04:37.232 --> 00:04:40.535
Judge Perry Nicosia accepted the plea.

00:04:41.005 --> 00:04:44.433
He said it was a bargain, 'in
the truest sense of the word',

00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:48.639
and that a jury conviction would likely
have meant a much harsher sentence.

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:54.020
And Nodier's own lawyer said that,
under the parole rules from 1985,

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:56.994
he could expect to serve
less than five years.

00:04:57.709 --> 00:05:00.339
Sheriff James Pohlmann
said he would have liked

00:05:00.339 --> 00:05:03.018
to see him spend the rest
of his life in prison.

00:05:03.832 --> 00:05:07.424
And John Doran, the detective
who'd reopened the case, said:

00:05:07.424 --> 00:05:09.818
"Some form of justice has been served.

00:05:10.297 --> 00:05:12.759
Ten years is better than none."

00:05:13.441 --> 00:05:18.636
On the eleventh of October, 2026, HBO
premiered a three-part series about the

00:05:18.636 --> 00:05:21.891
case: The Pet Cemetery
Murders: A Ghost Story.

00:05:22.186 --> 00:05:26.434
It's a Lightbox production, written
and directed by Philip Cairney.

00:05:26.727 --> 00:05:30.971
Parts two and three air on the
eighteenth and twenty-fifth of October.

00:05:31.407 --> 00:05:33.551
Its title says 'murders'.

00:05:34.013 --> 00:05:36.853
The legal record says manslaughter.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.081
One critic, at RogerEbert.com,
found the case itself thin,

00:05:41.081 --> 00:05:45.703
and said the series centres people who
earnestly believe a woman's ghost helped

00:05:45.703 --> 00:05:46.954
solve her murder.

00:05:47.407 --> 00:05:50.968
No DNA breakthrough
was ever reported here.

00:05:51.474 --> 00:05:54.589
The case was built on what witnesses said.

00:05:55.414 --> 00:05:57.958
And Brandon Nodier today?

00:05:58.535 --> 00:06:03.419
Biography.com reports that his
exact whereabouts are unknown.

00:06:03.871 --> 00:06:06.987
We found no public record of his release.

00:06:07.827 --> 00:06:10.462
So, here's where the record stands.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.917
Dorothy Thompson was sixty-one,
and fighting in court for her land,

00:06:14.917 --> 00:06:16.322
when she disappeared.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:21.579
Brandon Nodier, her groundskeeper,
pleaded guilty to manslaughter in 2014,

00:06:21.579 --> 00:06:23.859
twenty-nine years after her death.

00:06:24.363 --> 00:06:26.941
He was sentenced to ten years.

00:06:27.642 --> 00:06:30.648
Two witnesses said her ghost haunted them.

00:06:31.137 --> 00:06:35.440
Whatever you believe about that, what
ended this case was a witness who said he

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.212
was there, and a guilty plea.
